-- title:
--   Rigid-chart decomposition order is twice the place width (q=3)
-- statement:
--   **Base arithmetic data.** Let $q$ be a prime with $q=3$, let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and let $\ell_g$ be a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $q$ in the sense of `LiesOverPrime`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$; write $\kappa =$ `ResidueField A`. Let $W$ be a finite set of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M' = \kappa(j,\ j\circ q^{M'})$ over $\kappa$ (a place being a proper valuation subring containing the base field and with principal ideals), and assume (`hW`) that $W$ consists exactly of the supersingular places `ssPlaces q M' κ`: those $w$ which are rational (the structure map $\kappa \to$ residue field of $w$ is onto), satisfy the predicate `IsAffineGeomPlace`, and have $w.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathrm{jGeomGen})$ in `ssJSet q κ`, that is, every elliptic curve over $\kappa$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point. A place $s \in W$ is fixed. Further (`hle`) the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-base change $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ of the full modular function field of level $M'$ (generated over $\mathbb Q$ by the series $\mathrm{qExpand}\,d\,j$ for $d \mid M'$) is contained in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-base change of the function field of $X_H(q^2M')$ for $H = \mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$ the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$.
--
--   **Constant reduction.** $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$: a valuation subring $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ together with a surjective residue homomorphism onto the characteristic-$q$ modular function field whose kernel is the maximal ideal, meeting $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in $A$, compatible with the residue map of $A$, admitting for each nonzero $f$ a scalar $c$ with $c\cdot f$ integral of nonzero residue, and equipped with a degree-preserving map on places compatible with orders of elements. The hypothesis `hR₀` states that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose coefficientwise image lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, that element lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\kappa$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   **Small constants.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ an element of $A$. Write $A_0 = A \cap k_0$ (formally, the comap of $A$ along $k_0 \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$). The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` require $A_0$ to be a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, henselian, with algebraically closed residue field, and `hκ` requires every $a \in A$ to be congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to some element of $k_0 \cap A$.
--
--   **Auxiliary prime, roots of unity, uniformisers.** $\ell$ is a prime with $\ell \ge 3$, $\ell \ne q$, $\ell \nmid M'$; $\zeta_0 \in k_0$ is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; $\varpi_t \in k_0$ lies in $A$ and satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$ (`hϖt`). Moreover $K_b$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ with $K_b = \bot$, $A_b$ a valuation subring of $K_b$ whose elements are exactly those lying in $A$, and $\varpi_b$ a nonzero generator of the maximal ideal of $A_b$. Tameness (`htame`) asks that $\ell_g - 1$ be a unit of $A_0$, and $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity.
--
--   **Level data and the $j$-chart.** $H_1$ is the subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ equal to the intersection of $\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$ (units $\equiv 1$ modulo $q$) with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$, and $K_\ell$ is the $k_0$-base change inside $k_0((q))$ of the function field of $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$; $K_\ell$ is regarded as an $A_0$-algebra compatibly with $k_0$. The element $j_\ell \in K_\ell$ is the image of the $q$-expansion $j$ under coefficient extension, and is nonzero. The element $\varpi_t' \in A_0$ has image $\varpi_t$ in $k_0$. The $j$-finite chart algebra is $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A_0\,K_\ell\,j_\ell$, the subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $A_0[j_\ell]$, and $\mathrm{jChartFin}$ denotes $j_\ell$ viewed in it.
--
--   **The supersingular point $y$.** $y$ is a maximal ideal of the chart algebra containing the image of $\pi_0$ (`hϖy`), supersingular (`hss`): for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the chart algebra with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(\mathrm{jChartFin})$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`. The hypothesis `hover` says that $y$ lies over $s$: for every $g$ in the full modular function field of level $M'$ whose base change lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, which has poles only where $j$ has them (at every place $P$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, $\mathrm{ord}_P(j) \ge 0$ implies $\mathrm{ord}_P(g) \ge 0$), whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and which lies in $K_\ell$ and in the chart algebra, and for every $c \in k_0 \cap A$ whose residue in $\kappa$ equals $s.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of that $R_0$-residue, the difference $g - c$ lies in $y$. The hypothesis `hOrbit` says that any maximal ideal $y'$ of the chart algebra containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same lying-over condition is a translate of $y$: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a $k_0$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ with $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt}\,k_0\,q\,\xi\,q\,(q^2M')\,H_1\,\gamma^{-1}\,K_\ell\,\tau$ — i.e. $\tau$ realises on $q$-expansions the slash action of $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ on ratios of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$, tested through embeddings $k_0 \to \mathbb C$ sending $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$ — such that $\tau$ preserves the chart algebra and, for all chart elements $f$, $f \in y \iff \tau f \in y'$.
--
--   **The rigid chart (`hS1`).** $B_t$ is an $A_0$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$, $W_t$ a valuation subring of $K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`), and `hS1` is a conjunction of fourteen clauses: the chart algebra is contained in $B_t$; $K_\ell$ is the fraction field of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$ and $B_t/\pi_0 B_t$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t \cap k_0 = A_0$, the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is generated by the image of $\pi_0$, $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring, the centre of $W_t$ on the chart algebra is exactly $y$, and $W_t$ is the localisation of $B_t$ at its maximal ideal; the Drinfeld-curve clause, which for every algebra structure of $\mathbb F_{q^2} = \mathrm{GaloisField}\,q\,2$ on the residue field of $A_0$ provides a surjection $\rho$ from $B_t$ onto $\mathrm{DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing}\,q$ over that residue field, with kernel the elements of $B_t$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W_t$, compatible with $A_0 \to$ its residue field, and equivariant: for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ for $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ there is $c \in \mathbb F_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c) \in \mathrm{DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup}\,q$ (the kernel of $(g,c) \mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho \circ \tau = \mathrm{hAction}(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma,c) \circ \rho$ on $B_t$, with $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$, and with the linked scalar clause that the same $c$ is realised by the purely scalar element $(1,c)$ through some $\gamma' \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau'$ for $\gamma'^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$; stability of $B_t$ under all level automorphisms with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; transitivity, namely every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ receives, under some level automorphism coming from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, all elements of $B_t$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W_t$; and the divisibility clause that $b \in B_t$ is divisible by $\pi_0$ as soon as all its $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$-translates lie in the maximal ideal of $W_t$.
--
--   **Decomposition data (`hR7`, `hR5d`).** $n \ge 1$ is a natural number dividing $q+1$; $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau_0$ is a level automorphism for $\gamma_0^{-1}$ with $f \in W_t \iff \tau_0 f \in W_t$; $m \ge 1$ satisfies $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t' \cdot (\text{unit})$ in $A_0$; $\zeta_c$ is an element of the adic completion of $A_0$ with $\zeta_c^n = 1$ and $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$; and `ends` is a finite set of subrings of $K_\ell$. The hypothesis `hR7` (ten clauses, summarised here) requires: $\tau_0^n$ fixes $B_t$ pointwise while no smaller positive power does; every level automorphism coming from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with a power $\tau_0^k$, $k<n$; `ends` has exactly $q+1$ elements; level automorphisms from $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ permute `ends`, this action being transitive on ordered pairs, while $\tau_0$ stabilises each member; distinct members of `ends` are separated by an element which is a non-unit in one and a unit in the other; each $O \in$ `ends` is a local noetherian ring contained in $W_t$, containing the chart algebra, with $O \cap k_0 = A_0$ and with every element congruent to a constant from $A_0$ modulo non-units, and carries crossing data: elements $c_x, c_y \in O$ and a unit $u$ with $c_xc_y = \pi_0^m u$, an isomorphism $\iota$ of the adic completion of $O$ onto the crossing model $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}$ over the completion of $A_0$ with parameter $\pi_0^m$, i.e. $\widehat{A_0}[[U,V]]/(UV - \pi_0^m)$, carrying constants to constants and $c_x, c_y$ to unit multiples of $U$ and $V$, with $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, and an automorphism $\theta_0$ of the crossing model conjugate to $\tau_0$ under $\iota$ and satisfying $\theta_0(U) \equiv \zeta_c U$, $\theta_0(V) \equiv \zeta_c^{-1} V$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal; together with a valuation subring $W_x$ containing $O$ and the chart algebra, meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, possessing an element $t$ whose reduction is transcendental (any polynomial over $A_0$ vanishing at $t$ modulo the maximal ideal has all coefficients in the maximal ideal of $A_0$), with $c_x$ in and $c_y$ outside its maximal ideal, whose centre on the chart algebra is contained in $y$ but not equal to it; and a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_x \subseteq B_t \cap O$, stable under the level automorphisms from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, with all elements regular wherever $j_\ell$ is, and such that $O$ is the localisation of $B_x$ at its non-units. The last clause of `hR7` is a dichotomy: any valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, containing the chart algebra and with $y$ contained in its centre, either contains $B_t$ or contains some $O \in$ `ends` with all non-units of $O$ inside the maximal ideal of $V$. Finally `hR5d` requires that every level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which stabilises $y$ (in the sense that a chart element lies in $y$ if and only if its image does) also stabilises $W_t$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses,
--   $$n = 2\,\mathrm{placeWidthChar}\,q\,M'\,s,$$
--   that is, $n$ equals twice the natural-number quotient of $\mathrm{jWidthChar}\,q\,(s.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathrm{jGeomGen}))$ — which, since $q = 3$, is $6$ if that value of $j$ is $0$ and $1$ otherwise — by $\mathrm{placeRamificationJ}\,M'\,s = \big(\mathrm{ord}_s(\mathrm{jGeomGen} - s.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathrm{jGeomGen}))\big)_{\ge 0}$.
--
--   This is the width identity at a supersingular point of the semistable model of a modular curve: the order $n$ of the cyclic group of deck transformations acting faithfully on the rigid (exceptional) chart above the supersingular place $s$ is computed to be twice the modular width of $s$, in the $q = 3$ form of the statement, with level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ cut out by the congruences modulo $q$ and modulo a guard prime $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$. It feeds the construction of semistable integral models with supersingular charts, nodes and inertia data at level $q^2M'$ over the small constant field $k_0$, via the identification of the decomposition order with the order of the automorphism group of the corresponding supersingular elliptic curve with its cyclic $M'$-structure.
